        { heading: "Informational vs. Normative Influence", source: "Deutsch & Gerard (1955)", body: "<p>Two distinct motivations drive conformity. Informational social influence occurs when people look to others for guidance in ambiguous situations — they genuinely believe the group is correct. Normative social influence occurs when people conform to be liked or accepted by the group, even if they privately disagree.</p><p>Understanding which type of influence is operating is crucial for intervention. Informational influence can be reduced by increasing confidence in one's own judgment, while normative influence is reduced by creating safe spaces for dissent.</p>" },
        { heading: "Real-World Implications", source: "Zimbardo, P. (2007)", body: "<p>Social influence phenomena have profound real-world implications. In organizations, groupthink can lead to catastrophic decisions when dissent is suppressed. In legal contexts, eyewitness confidence can be swayed by co-witnesses. In politics, social proof shapes voting behavior.</p><p>The Abu Ghraib scandal and corporate fraud cases demonstrate how situational forces can override individual character. Philip Zimbardo's Lucifer Effect argues that good people can be led to do evil things under certain social conditions.</p>" }
